{"subjectId":"java","id":"generics","title":"Generics: Classes, Methods & Bounded Types","group":"Exceptions & Generics","order":39,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Generics let a class or method work with <em>any</em> type while the compiler still checks types — no casts, and type errors show up at compile time instead of as <code>ClassCastException</code> at runtime.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Type parameter</strong> — a placeholder type such as <code>T</code> in <code>class Box&lt;T&gt;</code>. Using the class with a concrete type argument, <code>Box&lt;String&gt;</code>, makes every <code>T</code> mean <code>String</code> for the compiler.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bulb\"></i>Why generics?</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    @SuppressWarnings({\"rawtypes\", \"unchecked\"})\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List raw = new ArrayList();            // pre-Java-5 style: a list of Object\n        raw.add(\"hello\");\n        raw.add(42);                           // compiler can't stop this\n        try {\n            for (Object o : raw) {\n                String s = (String) o;         // manual cast, fails at runtime\n                System.out.println(s.toUpperCase());\n            }\n        } catch (ClassCastException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"ClassCastException at runtime\");\n        }\n\n        List&lt;String&gt; typed = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        typed.add(\"hello\");\n        // typed.add(42);                      // compile error instead\n        String s = typed.get(0);               // no cast needed\n        System.out.println(s.length());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>HELLO\nClassCastException at runtime\n5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Generic classes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Box&lt;T&gt; {\n        private T value;\n        Box(T value) { this.value = value; }\n        T get() { return value; }\n        &lt;R&gt; Box&lt;R&gt; map(java.util.function.Function&lt;T, R&gt; f) { return new Box&lt;&gt;(f.apply(value)); }\n    }\n\n    record Pair&lt;A, B&gt;(A first, B second) {\n        Pair&lt;B, A&gt; swap() { return new Pair&lt;&gt;(second, first); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Box&lt;String&gt; name = new Box&lt;&gt;(\"generics\");      // diamond: type inferred\n        Box&lt;Integer&gt; len = name.map(String::length);\n        System.out.println(name.get() + \" \" + len.get());\n\n        Pair&lt;String, Integer&gt; p = new Pair&lt;&gt;(\"age\", 30);\n        System.out.println(p.swap());\n        var q = new Pair&lt;&gt;(1.5, true);                  // Pair&lt;Double, Boolean&gt;\n        System.out.println(q.first() + \" \" + q.second());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>generics 8\nPair[first=30, second=age]\n1.5 true</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Convention</th><th>Stands for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>T</code>, <code>U</code>, <code>V</code></td><td>Type (first, second, third)</td></tr><tr><td><code>E</code></td><td>Element (collections)</td></tr><tr><td><code>K</code>, <code>V</code></td><td>Key, Value (maps)</td></tr><tr><td><code>R</code></td><td>Return / result type</td></tr><tr><td><code>N</code></td><td>Number</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-function\"></i>Generic methods</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static &lt;T&gt; T firstOr(List&lt;T&gt; list, T fallback) {       // &lt;T&gt; before the return type\n        return list.isEmpty() ? fallback : list.get(0);\n    }\n\n    static &lt;K, V&gt; Map&lt;V, K&gt; invert(Map&lt;K, V&gt; map) {\n        Map&lt;V, K&gt; out = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();\n        map.forEach((k, v) -&gt; out.put(v, k));\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(firstOr(List.of(\"a\", \"b\"), \"none\"));\n        System.out.println(firstOr(List.&lt;Integer&gt;of(), -1));     // explicit type witness\n        System.out.println(invert(Map.of(\"one\", 1, \"two\", 2)));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>a\n-1\n{1=one, 2=two}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-to-up\"></i>Bounded type parameters</h3>\n<p><code>&lt;T extends Bound&gt;</code> restricts <code>T</code> to <code>Bound</code> or its subtypes — and in return lets you call <code>Bound</code>'s methods on a <code>T</code>. (<code>extends</code> is used for both classes and interfaces; multiple bounds: <code>&lt;T extends Number &amp; Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>, class first.)</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static &lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt; T max(List&lt;T&gt; items) {\n        T best = items.get(0);\n        for (T item : items)\n            if (item.compareTo(best) &gt; 0) best = item;   // compareTo allowed thanks to the bound\n        return best;\n    }\n\n    static &lt;N extends Number&gt; double sum(List&lt;N&gt; nums) {\n        double total = 0;\n        for (N n : nums) total += n.doubleValue();       // Number's method\n        return total;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(max(List.of(3, 9, 4)));\n        System.out.println(max(List.of(\"pear\", \"apple\", \"zucchini\")));\n        System.out.println(sum(List.of(1, 2.5, 3L)));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>9\nzucchini\n6.5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-circle\"></i>Primitives and restrictions</h3>\n<ul><li>Type arguments must be reference types: <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> is illegal — use <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> (autoboxing does the conversion).</li><li>A static field or static method can't use the class's <code>T</code> (there's one static copy for all <code>Box&lt;X&gt;</code>), though a static method can declare its own <code>&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li><li>You can't create <code>new T()</code>, <code>new T[n]</code>, or <code>new List&lt;String&gt;[n]</code> — see type erasure in the next topic.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Raw types</div><p><strong>Raw types</strong> (<code>List</code> instead of <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code>) exist only for pre-Java-5 compatibility. They switch off generic type checking for that variable and produce <code>rawtypes</code>/<code>unchecked</code> warnings. Never use them in new code; use <code>List&lt;?&gt;</code> if you really mean \"a list of unknown type\".</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are generics and why use them?</summary><div><p>Parameterised types that let one class/method work with many types while keeping compile-time type safety. Benefits: errors caught at compile time, no explicit casts, and reusable, self-documenting APIs (<code>Map&lt;String, List&lt;Order&gt;&gt;</code>).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does &lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt; mean?</summary><div><p><code>T</code> can be any type that is comparable to itself, so the method may call <code>a.compareTo(b)</code> on two <code>T</code>s. The fully flexible version used by the JDK is <code>&lt;T extends Comparable&lt;? super T&gt;&gt;</code>, which also accepts types whose <code>compareTo</code> is inherited from a supertype.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the diamond operator?</summary><div><p><code>&lt;&gt;</code> in <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()</code> (Java 7+): the compiler infers the type arguments from the context, so you don't repeat <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt;</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}